Law thirty eight of the forty eight laws of power is about the practical wisdom of conformity. Robert Greene argues that the world is full of people who hold conventional beliefs and who are deeply uncomfortable with those who openly challenge or deviate from those beliefs. this discomfort is not trivial. it produces social costs that can be significant and in some contexts devastating. the person who openly displays unconventional thinking, who challenges prevailing assumptions or flaunts beliefs that differ from those around them, pays a price in social friction, suspicion, and opposition that can far exceed whatever satisfaction the open expression of those beliefs provides. Greene's advice is not to abandon unconventional thinking but to protect it, to keep it private, and to conform publicly to the norms and expectations of the social environment you inhabit.
the psychological principle at the heart of this law is that most people feel threatened by perspectives that challenge their own. this is not primarily an intellectual reaction but an emotional one. when someone openly holds and expresses beliefs that differ significantly from the prevailing consensus, those around them experience it as a form of implicit criticism. if you are right, they are wrong. if your approach is better, theirs is worse. even without any explicit judgment on your part, the visible difference in perspective creates a kind of challenge that triggers defensiveness. and defensive people work to protect themselves by dismissing, marginalizing, or actively opposing the source of the challenge.
Greene opens with examples from the history of intellectual and religious nonconformity to illustrate the costs of open deviation. in the history of science and philosophy, countless thinkers who openly challenged prevailing beliefs paid enormous prices. they were ostracized, persecuted, or destroyed by institutions and social forces that felt threatened by their ideas. in contrast, thinkers who maintained the appearance of conformity while quietly developing and testing their heterodox ideas in private were often able to accomplish far more precisely because they avoided the friction that open nonconformity generates. they worked within the system while privately building the body of thought that would eventually change it.
the core practical advice of this law is about where to draw the line between private and public expression. in your private life, in the company of people who have demonstrated their trustworthiness and their genuine openness to different perspectives, you can think and speak as you actually believe. this private space is essential. it is where genuine thinking happens, where ideas are tested, and where the inner life that makes you who you are is sustained. but in public, in the company of people whose response to nonconformity would be costly, the wise practitioner presents the version of themselves that creates the least friction. they express the conventional views, they use the expected language, and they give no visible indication that they see the world differently.
Greene is careful to clarify that this is not the same as abandoning your beliefs or pretending to believe things you do not. it is about managing where and when you express them. the distinction between thinking and behaving is the key. you can think whatever you like, hold whatever beliefs, develop whatever private understanding of the world you choose. none of that is anyone else's business. but behavior, the public face, the words and actions that others observe and respond to, is different. behavior has social consequences, and managing those consequences intelligently means conforming enough to avoid unnecessary friction while maintaining the private freedom that genuine thinking requires.
there is also a strategic dimension to this law that goes beyond the management of social friction. the person who appears to conform to prevailing norms and beliefs is often able to move through their environment with much greater ease and access than the person who is known as a nonconformist. they are trusted with information and opportunities that the known contrarian would be denied. they have relationships and alliances that the open rebel would find impossible to maintain. and these resources, relationships, and access are precisely what they need to eventually act on the private beliefs they have been protecting.
Greene draws a clear distinction between this law and the trap of total conformity. he is not advising that you become a thoroughgoing social conformist who never challenges anything and whose inner and outer life are completely aligned with prevailing norms. that kind of conformity is itself a form of intellectual and personal poverty. the goal is not to become someone who cannot think beyond the consensus but to become someone who can protect the space for unconventional thinking by managing how much of it is visible in contexts where its visibility would be costly.
there is a creative tension in this law that Greene acknowledges. the most original and most powerful thinking often requires a kind of inner distance from prevailing assumptions, a willingness to question things that most people accept without examination. this inner distance is what produces genuine insight. but maintaining it requires protecting it from the social pressure that grinds it down over time in those who are too transparently nonconformist. the privacy of your thinking is the condition of its freedom, and the public performance of conformity is the price of that privacy.
Greene also notes the importance of reading your specific environment carefully. the degree of conformity that is necessary varies enormously between different contexts. some environments are genuinely tolerant of diverse perspectives and actually reward unconventional thinking. in those environments, the cost of open nonconformity is low and the calculation changes. other environments are deeply intolerant of any deviation from the prevailing consensus, and in those environments the cost of open difference is very high. the wise practitioner reads their environment accurately and calibrates their degree of public conformity to the actual level of risk that visibility would create.
in conclusion, law thirty eight teaches that the protection of your inner freedom requires the management of your outer expression. by conforming publicly to the norms and expectations of the environments you inhabit, you avoid the social costs that open nonconformity generates, preserve your access to the relationships and resources that power requires, and protect the private space where genuine and unconventional thinking can continue without interference. think as you like. but behave like others. the inner freedom is worth the outer performance.
